    "title": "Unit tests on Windows with `DEBUG_LOCKORDER` have been broken for a while",
    "body": "I was testing some socket stuff in a Windows VM and noticed unit tests don't pass for a mingw build with `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug`. The regression was introduced in #35614. To reproduce, build with `DEBUG_LOCKORDER` and run that HTTP server test:\n```\ntest_bitcoin --run_test=httpserver_tests/http_socket_error_tests\n```\n\nHaven't looked into the bug myself, but apparently it's trivial to fix. See slop below.\n\n#### LLM summary of the issue\n\nIn several socket paths, `WSAGetLastError()` is read after leaving the `LOCK(m_sock_mutex)` scope that wrapped the `send()`/`recv()` call. With `DEBUG_LOCKORDER`, releasing the lock runs `pop_lock()` (`src/sync.cpp:212`, `:215`), which calls `std::this_thread::get_id()`. With the mingw-w64 posix thread model, that call resets the Windows last error to 0. A `WSAEWOULDBLOCK` is then read as error 0, which `IOErrorIsPermanent()` treats as permanent, so the connection is dropped.\n\n*Suggested fix*. Read `WSAGetLastError()` inside the lock scope, right after the socket call. With that change, the test passes on Windows Debug.",
